---
// bronzing-loop — why a disease of too-little cortisol darkens the skin. In
// primary adrenal failure (Addison's) the adrenal cannot make cortisol, so the
// pituitary never gets the negative-feedback signal to stop. It pumps out more
// POMC, and therefore more ACTH. ACTH's first thirteen residues ARE α-MSH, so a
// flood of ACTH is also a flood of melanocortin at MC1R in the skin — which
// answers by making eumelanin. The low-cortisol signal and the dark skin are
// the same loop, read at two ends. Schematic; SVG-only, wrap in <Figure>.
---

<svg
  class="bl-fig"
  viewBox="0 0 640 318"
  role="img"
  aria-labelledby="bl-t bl-d"
  xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
>
  <title id="bl-t">Why Addison's disease bronzes the skin</title>
  <desc id="bl-d"
    >In primary adrenal failure the adrenal gland cannot make cortisol. Without
    cortisol the pituitary loses its negative-feedback brake and releases far
    more POMC and therefore ACTH. Because the first thirteen amino acids of ACTH
    are identical to alpha-MSH, this flood of ACTH is also a flood of
    melanocortin signal at MC1R in the skin, which responds by making eumelanin —
    so the skin darkens. The missing cortisol and the darkened skin are two ends
    of the same loop.</desc
  >

  <!-- adrenal: the failure -->
  <rect class="card miss" x="20" y="36" width="178" height="62" rx="11" />
  <text x="109" y="62" text-anchor="middle" class="card-t miss-t">Adrenal fails</text>
  <text x="109" y="80" text-anchor="middle" class="sub">little or no cortisol</text>

  <!-- arrow down: lost feedback -->
  <path class="flow" d="M109 98 V150" marker-end="url(#bl-arr)" />
  <text x="118" y="128" class="side">no cortisol →</text>
  <text x="118" y="142" class="side">no "stop" signal</text>

  <!-- pituitary: ramps up -->
  <rect class="card" x="20" y="152" width="178" height="62" rx="11" />
  <text x="109" y="178" text-anchor="middle" class="card-t">Pituitary ramps up</text>
  <text x="109" y="196" text-anchor="middle" class="sub">POMC → ACTH surges</text>

  <!-- arrow right: ACTH carries a-MSH -->
  <path class="flow accent-flow" d="M198 183 H300" marker-end="url(#bl-arr-a)" />
  <text x="249" y="172" text-anchor="middle" class="keytxt accent-t">ACTH 1–13 = α-MSH</text>
  <text x="249" y="205" text-anchor="middle" class="sub">melanocortin signal</text>

  <!-- MC1R in skin -->
  <rect class="card accent" x="302" y="152" width="170" height="62" rx="11" />
  <text x="387" y="178" text-anchor="middle" class="card-t accent-t">MC1R in skin</text>
  <text x="387" y="196" text-anchor="middle" class="sub">reads it as "make pigment"</text>

  <!-- arrow right: eumelanin -->
  <path class="flow accent-flow" d="M472 183 H520" marker-end="url(#bl-arr-a)" />

  <!-- bronzing -->
  <rect class="card accent" x="522" y="152" width="98" height="62" rx="11" />
  <text x="571" y="178" text-anchor="middle" class="card-t accent-t">Eumelanin</text>
  <text x="571" y="196" text-anchor="middle" class="sub">skin bronzes</text>

  <!-- the punchline band -->
  <rect class="band" x="20" y="250" width="600" height="48" rx="12" />
  <text x="40" y="272" class="band-t">The missing cortisol and the darkened skin are the same loop —</text>
  <text x="40" y="289" class="band-t">read at two ends. The bronzing is the diagnosis, written on the body.</text>

  <defs>
    <marker id="bl-arr" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
      <path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z" fill="var(--text-muted)" />
    </marker>
    <marker id="bl-arr-a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
      <path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z" fill="var(--accent)" />
    </marker>
  </defs>
</svg>

